{
  "answer": "Based on the Viking Lander 1 binned and splined meteorology dataset (Murphy, Leovy & Tillman, University of Washington), the maximum single-sol diurnal temperature range during the first 50 sols at Chryse Planitia was about 64 K, reached on sol 17: the air temperature swung from a pre-dawn minimum of about 187 K (-86 C) to an early-afternoon maximum of about 251 K (-22 C). Across sols 1-50 the diurnal range averaged about 57 K, with the largest swings clustering in sols 14-18 (62-64 K). This is consistent with the first published results, which reported temperatures fluctuating from 188 K to an estimated maximum of 244 K over the first 4 sols (Hess et al., Science, 27 Aug 1976). [Viking Lander 1 binned data](http://www-k12.atmos.washington.edu/k12/mars/data/vl1/part1.html) [Hess et al., Science 193, 788-791](https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.193.4255.788)

The first 50 sols correspond to solar longitude Ls about 97-124 deg, i.e. northern summer, when dust opacity sits at its seasonal minimum. Viking Lander camera images of the Sun give the total normal optical depth at the site from sol 6 onward (NASA PDS dataset VL1/VL2-M-LCS-5-ATMOS-OPTICAL-DEPTH-V1.0). When no dust storm is present the optical depth is typically about 0.5, and opacities are minimum during northern spring and summer (Ls 0-180 deg) and maximum during southern spring and summer, when most dust storms occur (Appelbaum & Flood, NASA TM-103623, 1990). No dust storm occurred during sols 1-50, so tau stayed low and nearly flat through the whole window. [PDS atmospheric opacity dataset](https://atmos.nmsu.edu/PDS4BETA/MARS/pathfinder/atm_opacity.html) [Appelbaum & Flood, NASA TM-103623](https://archive.org/stream/NASA_NTRS_Archive_19910005804/NASA_NTRS_Archive_19910005804_djvu.txt)

Correlation: within sols 1-50 there is no strong day-to-day correlation between dust opacity and the diurnal temperature range, precisely because opacity barely varied in that window - the range was driven by the diurnal insolation cycle, not by changes in tau. The correlation is instead seasonal and inverse: the large 57-64 K ranges are themselves the signature of the opacity minimum. Clear skies let the surface heat strongly by day and cool unimpeded by radiation at night, maximising the swing. When opacity later rose sharply - during the 1977 global dust storms tau exceeded 1 and reached about 3.6 - airborne dust cut the sunlight reaching the surface by day while absorbing surface-emitted infrared and re-radiating it at night, which collapses the diurnal range. In short: maximum range about 64 K (sol 17); dust opacity low and steady at about 0.35-0.5 through sols 1-50; and the two are linked seasonally (low opacity enables the large range) rather than by any day-to-day co-variation inside the 50-sol window."
}

{
  "answer": "Between sols 31 and 60 of the Viking 1 mission at Chryse Planitia (northern summer, solar longitude Ls about 111.1 to 125.5 degrees), the average diurnal temperature range was about 56.4 K (about 56 degrees C). I computed this directly from the archived binned-and-splined Viking Lander 1 meteorology dataset (Murphy, Leovy and Tillman, 25 hourly bins per sol, NASA PDS vl_mbin): for each of the 30 sols I took the daily maximum minus the daily minimum of the 1.6-metre air temperature, then averaged the 30 daily ranges. The per-sol ranges run from 53.5 K (sol 31) to 58.7 K (sol 54), with a median of 56.6 K, so the 56.4 K mean is stable rather than driven by one outlier sol. Typical daily extremes in the window were a pre-dawn minimum near -85 C and an early-afternoon maximum near -28 C (mean of the 30 daily minima about -85.2 C, mean of the 30 daily maxima about -28.9 C). The peak atmospheric pressure recorded in the same window was 7.37 mbar, on sol 32. Pressures then drifted steadily downward through the window, from about 7.36 mbar at sol 31 to about 7.06-7.11 mbar by sols 59-60, consistent with the seasonal pressure decline as the northern summer progressed; for comparison, Hess et al. reported a mean pressure of 7.65 mbar with a small diurnal amplitude of about 0.1 mbar over the first 4 sols. Sources: the binned Viking Lander 1 data table (http://www-k12.atmos.washington.edu/k12/mars/data/vl1/part1.html), the NMSU/PDS Viking Lander 1 archive index (https://atmos.nmsu.edu/data_and_services/atmospheres_data/MARS/pathfinder/viking1.html), and Hess et al., Science 193, 788-791 (1976) (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.193.4255.788). One caveat: these figures come from hourly bin-averaged data, so the true instantaneous (minute-scale) extremes could differ slightly from the binned values."
}
